src/RESPLE.cpp -- the RESPLE node itself.

One patch per upstream file, so this one patch carries every change the bridge
makes to RESPLE.cpp regardless of concern. To keep that from hiding the "why",
each hunk explains itself *in the source*, tagged `resple_bridge:` -- after
patching, `grep -n 'resple_bridge:' src/RESPLE.cpp` enumerates the entire local
delta with its rationale, in the staged file a reader is actually looking at.
This header is only an index; the reasoning has one home, at the sites.

1. Visibility. `private:` -> `public:` so cpp/bindings.cpp can inject sensor
   data into RESPLE's own buffers and read spline state. No behavior change.
2. Finish condition. processData()'s `while (true)` gets a state-based exit:
   the first pass that both began with input closed and moved nothing.
3. Queue discipline. Pop under the queues' own mutex; notify the bridge on
   every dequeue, which is what releases producer backpressure.
4. ikd-tree quiescence at each measurement-batch boundary (barrier added by
   02-ikd-tree.patch).
5. Deterministic initialization(): the initial map is built only from the
   *complete* fixed 100 ms window, and propRCP(start_t_ns) moves below both
   retry gates so it runs exactly once. Both were producer/worker races that
   several LiDARs make easy to hit, because they make the number of
   initialization passes scheduling-dependent. Single-LiDAR replay never
   entered the retry path, so its trajectory is bit-identical across this
   change.
6. An actionable failure when a complete 100 ms window holds fewer than the
   required 100 downsampled points; more input cannot change a closed window.

